1. What is a literal type?

A literal type allows only one specific value.

let role: "admin";

role = "admin"; // Valid
role = "user";  // Error

Here, role can only contain "admin".

2. What is a literal union?

A literal union allows multiple specific values using the | operator.

type Role = "admin" | "user" | "guest";

let role: Role;

role = "admin";   // Valid
role = "user";    // Valid
role = "guest";   // Valid
role = "manager"; // Error

Only the three specified values are allowed.

3. Why use literal types?

Literal types restrict values and help catch invalid assignments.

type Status = "pending" | "success" | "failed";

function updateStatus(status: Status) {
    console.log(status);
}

updateStatus("success"); // Valid
updateStatus("failed");  // Valid
updateStatus("done");    // Error

Benefit: TypeScript catches invalid values before your code runs.

4. What is the difference between string and "admin"?
// General string type
let username: string;

username = "Bhargav"; // Valid
username = "admin";   // Valid
username = "123";     // Valid

// Literal type
let role: "admin";

role = "admin"; // Valid
role = "user";  // Error

Key difference: string accepts any string, whereas "admin" accepts only that exact string.

5. What does as const do?

as const preserves literal types and makes object properties readonly at the type level.

const user = {
    name: "Bhargav",
    role: "admin"
} as const;

user.role = "user"; // Error
user.name = "Rahul"; // Error

Without as const, TypeScript generally infers the properties as string, allowing reassignment.

const user = {
    name: "Bhargav",
    role: "admin"
};

user.role = "user"; // Valid


 as const provides compile-time protection. It does not freeze the object at runtime.
what does it mean?

It means as const prevents you from modifying values in TypeScript, but it doesn't actually lock the object in JavaScript.

1. Compile-time protection
const user = {
    name: "Bhargav",
    role: "admin"
} as const;

user.role = "guest"; // TypeScript error

TypeScript detects the error before your code runs because role is readonly.

2. Runtime protection

When TypeScript is compiled into JavaScript, as const disappears.

The resulting JavaScript is essentially:

const user = {
    name: "Bhargav",
    role: "admin"
};

user.role = "guest"; // Works!
console.log(user.role); // "guest"

JavaScript doesn't know anything about as const.

3. What if you actually want to prevent modifications?

Use Object.freeze().

const user = Object.freeze({
    name: "Bhargav",
    role: "admin"
});

user.role = "guest"; // TypeScript error
